laurel 2988: the stuck-CPU report says whether the CPU is inside the kernel on an EL0 trap, and which; both kernels. docs/laurel_sprout/watchdog.md.

[V 2026-11-03 17:56:16, logs/hang-175616.txt] The first catch of 2986: CPU 5 silent 19.5 s with its SGI pending, no interrupt handler open, the last
interrupt from EL0 of iconservicesagent. The two lines could not tell a CPU stalled on one user instruction (no interrupt can come in) from a kernel path
entered from EL0 that holds interrupts off. Each exception from EL0 now stamps its CPU (time, ESR, FAR, user pc, thread) in sleh_synchronous, and the
stamp closes when the thread goes back to EL0 (thread_exception_return, the end of sleh_synchronous): post/9017 holds the sleh.c calls for iOS 26.
A third report line: "is in the kernel since an EL0 trap N ms ago: class (name) esr far user pc" or "has no EL0 trap open (in EL0, or the trap ended)",
the redistributor's whole pending and active words (a PPI or SGI taken and never ended shows as active), and the dyld shared cache slide of the task on
that CPU (the slide is random per boot, so a user pc had no image before). iOS 16: the functions exist, nothing calls them yet (its sleh.c hunk is in 2986).
Cost: five stores per EL0 exception. No new line at boot; nothing to turn off.

